    {
      "ordinal": 13,
      "depth": 1,
      "kind": "rule",
      "number": "7.1",
      "title": "Historical Sources and Datation",
      "body": "Punch Shields are designed to strike with the edge. The handles and straps allow you to wear them in a striking position. Their shape may vary depending on the time and place. Most common shapes are rectangular, trapezoid and oval.\nTheir ultimate evolution is the Pavese of the 15th century. This type of Shield has a rectangular shape and a gutter in the middle.\nAll shapes except the Pavese are Approved for 14th century, Transitional and 15th century armors.\nPavese shape is Approved for 15th century armors.\nPavese shape is Denied for 14th century and Transitional armors.\n\nCastello di Avio - 1350 - Italy\n\nShield of Order Teutonicus Great Master - 1320 - Germany\n\nDeutsches Historisches Museum (Berlin) - 1480 - Germany Wien Museum - 1480 - Austria\n\nPaduan Bible - 1400 - Italy",
      "page_from": 9,
      "page_to": 10,
      "stable_key": "shields-and-bucklers:7.1",
